 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time Complexity:** $O(N_{iters} \cdot N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples} \cdot D)$, where $D$ is the MLP depth and width. The dominant cost is the MLP evaluation for each sample point [S5, S10, unofficial].
-**Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples} \cdot D)$ for storing intermediate activations during backpropagation, plus $O(N_{iters})$ for optimizer states [S5, S10, unofficial]. VRAM overflow is a common pitfall, mitigated by chunking ray batches [S5, S16, unofficial].
-**Measured Performance:** Training typically requires 100,000 iterations. On a single GPU, this can take several hours to days depending on resolution and scene complexity [S11, S18, unofficial].
-**Scaling:** Performance scales with batch size ($N_{rand}$) up to VRAM limits. Larger batch sizes improve gradient stability but reduce iterations per second due to data loading overhead [S16, unofficial].
-**Strengths:** Simple to implement, differentiable end-to-end, produces high-quality novel views.
-**Limitations:** Slow training and rendering, prone to artifacts in sparse views, requires careful hyperparameter tuning [S1][S18, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{iters} \cdot N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples} \cdot C)$, where $N_{iters}$ is the number of iterations, $N_{rand}$ is the batch size (rays per iteration), $N_{samples}$ is the number of samples per ray, and $C$ is the cost of the network forward pass [S5, S15, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples} \cdot D)$, where $D$ is the dimensionality of the network activations. VRAM usage is a primary constraint, often mitigated by chunking rays [S5, S16, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** On the Blender dataset (800x800), training typically takes 100,000 iterations [S11, unofficial]. On LLFF with 3 views, using 50 target rays yields PSNR 20.33, SSIM 0.661, LPIPS 0.276; increasing to 200 rays degrades performance [S21].
+*   **Scaling:** Batch size is a trade-off; larger batches (e.g., $2^{14}$) hinder data loading performance, while smaller batches (e.g., $2^{10}$) cap iterations per second [S16, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, differentiable, produces high-quality novel views.
+*   **Limitations:** Slow convergence, high memory usage, sensitive to hyperparameters (learning rate, batch size), prone to artifacts (floaters) in sparse views [S1][S18, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When high-quality novel view synthesis is required and sufficient computational resources are available.
+*   **When not to use:** When real-time rendering is required or when input data is extremely sparse and uncalibrated without additional priors.
 
 ## Background
-The NeRF training loop was introduced in the seminal paper "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S5, S10, unofficial]. It builds on the concept of implicit neural representations for 3D scenes [S10, unofficial]. The method has become a standard baseline for novel view synthesis and has inspired numerous variants and optimizations [S25].
+The NeRF training loop was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 in the paper "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" [S5, S10, unofficial]. It builds on the concept of implicit neural representations for 3D scenes [S1][S3, unofficial]. The method was initially implemented in TensorFlow [S5, unofficial] and later ported to PyTorch [S6, unofficial] and JAX [S13, unofficial][S25]. It has become the standard baseline for neural radiance field optimization, with numerous variants and optimizations developed since its inception [S1][S2, S4, S7, S11, S13, S14, S15, S16, S17, S18, S19, S20, unofficial][S21, S22, S23][S24, unofficial][S25][S26, unofficial][S27, S28, S29].
 
